    "Why It Exists": "- Developers fail to implement consistent error messages\n- Different password validation logic for existing vs non-existing users\n- Response time differences based on user existence\n- User existence checking in password recovery\n- Verbose error messages disclosing account information\n- Lack of security code review\n- Third-party libraries with default behaviors",
    "Attacker Interest": "Medium to High",
    "Discovery Methods": "- Error message analysis (invalid user vs invalid password)\n- Response time analysis (timing attacks)\n- HTTP status code comparison (404 vs 401)\n- Password reset form testing\n- Account recovery page testing\n- API response analysis\n- Feedback message differentiation\n- Login attempt behavior analysis",
    "Common Services": "- Web applications\n- Email systems\n- Identity management systems\n- APIs\n- Cloud platforms\n- Mobile applications\n- Collaboration tools",
    "Common Attacker Actions": "- Username enumeration for targeted attacks\n- Building username lists for spraying attacks\n- Identifying administrator accounts\n- Social engineering with confirmed usernames\n- Targeted phishing campaigns\n- Brute-force attacks against identified users",
    "Related MITRE Techniques": "- T1087 — Account Discovery\n- T1087.001 — Local Account Discovery\n- T1087.002 — Domain Account Discovery\n- T1087.004 — Cloud Account Discovery\n- T1598 — Phishing for Information",
    "Kill Chain Relevance": "- Reconnaissance (username identification)\n- Initial Access (targeted attacks against identified users)",
    "Potential Impact": "- Confirmed valid usernames enabling targeted attacks\n- Reduced attack time through targeted focus\n- Social engineering targeting confirmed accounts\n- Credential stuffing against identified users\n- Privacy violation (confirming user existence)",
    "Real World Abuse": "",
    "Scenario 1": "Attacker attempts login with \"admin@company.com\". Receives \"Invalid username or password\". Attempts \"admin1@company.com\". Receives \"Incorrect password\". Enumeration successful—admin1 exists. Initiates phishing campaign specifically targeting admin1.",
    "Scenario 2": "Password reset form returns \"If account exists, password reset link sent\". Attacker submits dozens of email addresses. Checks mail inbox. Some trigger confirmation emails, others don't. Has confirmed list of actual company emails. Uses list for targeted phishing.",
    "Detection Opportunities": "- Multiple login attempts with invalid usernames\n- Unusual username patterns in failed login attempts\n- Password reset attempts with multiple usernames\n- Account discovery tools querying account existence\n- Timing analysis attacks against login endpoint\n- Bulk email submission to password reset form",
    "Security Recommendations": "- Return identical error messages for invalid user and invalid password\n- Implement consistent response times (add random delays if needed)\n- Use same HTTP status code (401) for authentication failures\n- Avoid disclosing account existence in any response\n- Implement rate limiting on enumeration attacks\n- Use generic error messages: \"Invalid credentials\"\n- Require email verification before account recovery\n- Implement CAPTCHA on repeated failure attempts\n- Monitor for account enumeration attacks\n- Test authentication error messages during code review\n- Implement accounts lockout preventing brute-force enumeration",
    "Compliance References": "- OWASP: Testing for Weak Authentication\n- CIS Controls: 5.1",
    "ETH Narrative": "*Red Team Perspective:* \"Invalid username\" vs \"Invalid password\" seems like minor UX difference. For you it's reconnaissance gold. One message confirms the user exists; the other confirms they don't. You build a spreadsheet of valid usernames in seconds. Now you target them specifically—phishing, credential stuffing, social engineering. The organization's security team doesn't realize they're leaking enumeration data with every failed login attempt.\n\n---"
