    "Common Attacker Actions": "- Password reset token forgery\n- Token interception and reuse\n- Token prediction and valid token enumeration\n- Account takeover via reset token\n- Privilege escalation (admin account reset)\n- Lateral movement (compromised account access)",
    "Related MITRE Techniques": "- T1110 — Brute Force\n- T1078 — Valid Accounts\n- T1187 — Forced Authentication\n- T1491.001 — Defacement",
    "Kill Chain Relevance": "- Initial Access (account takeover)\n- Persistence (compromised account)\n- Privilege Escalation (admin account reset)\n- Defense Evasion (legitimate password reset appearing legitimate)",
    "Potential Impact": "- Unauthorized account takeover\n- Complete account compromise\n- Privilege escalation to administrative accounts\n- Data exfiltration using compromised account\n- Long-term persistence\n- Regulatory non-compliance",
    "Real World Abuse": "",
    "Scenario 1": "Attacker initiates password reset for victim's email. Intercepts reset email. Token in URL: \"/reset?token=123456\". Attempts \"/reset?token=123457\", \"/reset?token=123458\". Token 123459 is valid. Attacker changes password. Account compromised.",
    "Scenario 2": "Password reset token generated from username hash. Attacker calculates hash for admin account. Generates expected token. Submits token to reset endpoint. Endpoint accepts it. Attacker sets new password. Admin account compromised.",
    "Scenario 3": "Password reset email sent. Attacker compromises email account before user reads it. Clicks reset link. Sets new password. User now cannot access account. Attacker maintains persistence until new password set.",
    "Detection Opportunities": "- Multiple password reset attempts in short timeframe\n- Password reset tokens appearing in logs\n- Token reuse attempts\n- Successful password resets without user request\n- Password reset followed by unusual account activity\n- Rapid password changes across multiple accounts\n- Token brute-force attempts",
    "Security Recommendations": "- Generate reset tokens using cryptographically secure randomness (minimum 128-bit entropy)\n- Token should be unique, non-sequential, non-predictable\n- Token expires after 15-30 minutes\n- Token valid for single use only\n- Token bound to user (cannot reset different user with same token)\n- Require email verification before reset\n- Invalidate all active password reset tokens when password changed\n- Implement rate limiting on password reset requests\n- Require authentication for password reset (security questions, SMS)\n- Use time-limited links with secure token generation\n- Log all password reset attempts\n- Notify user of password reset attempt\n- Implement multi-factor verification for sensitive accounts\n- Avoid sending token in email URL (one-time links only)",
