    "Common Attacker Actions": "- Credential spraying attacks\n- Dictionary attacks\n- Brute-force password attacks\n- Credential stuffing from breach databases\n- Unlimited password guessing\n- Automation of authentication attacks",
    "Related MITRE Techniques": "- T1110 — Brute Force\n- T1110.001 — Password Guessing\n- T1110.004 — Credential Stuffing\n- T1078 — Valid Accounts",
    "Kill Chain Relevance": "- Initial Access (credential-based entry through brute-force)",
    "Potential Impact": "- Successful account compromise through brute-force\n- Unlimited attack attempts without detection\n- Long-term persistence using compromised credentials\n- Lateral movement through compromised accounts\n- Privilege escalation using administrator credentials",
    "Real World Abuse": "",
    "Scenario 1": "Legacy application has no account lockout. Attacker conducts credential spraying against employee list. Tests password \"Welcome123\" against 500 employee accounts. No lockout. Ten accounts accept credential. Attacker logs into ten compromised accounts.",
    "Scenario 2": "API endpoint lacks rate limiting. Attacker builds password list from rockyou.txt. Conducts brute-force attack against API. Tests every password until one matches. No lockout mechanism stops attack. Takes 12 hours but successfully guesses admin password.",
    "Detection Opportunities": "- Large number of failed authentication attempts from single source\n- Distributed failed authentication attempts (credential spraying pattern)\n- Successful login after many failed attempts\n- Unusual authentication patterns suggesting brute-force\n- Password guessing attempts visible in authentication logs\n- Rate limiting bypass indicators",
    "Security Recommendations": "- Implement account lockout after 5 failed attempts\n- Lockout duration: 15-30 minutes minimum\n- Implement exponential backoff (increase lockout duration after repeated locks)\n- Lockout applies across all authentication methods\n- Log all authentication attempts\n- Alert on account lockout\n- Implement rate limiting on authentication endpoint\n- Use CAPTCHA after repeated failures\n- Implement distributed rate limiting (account-based, IP-based, global)\n- Require manual unlock for sensitive accounts\n- Notify user when account locked\n- Implement temporary password or MFA fallback\n- Monitor for credential spraying patterns",
